WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the purpose of the CPU?', 'To fetch, decode and execute instructions, processing data to run programs.', 1),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What does the fetch–decode–execute cycle do?', 'The CPU repeatedly fetches an instruction from memory, decodes it to work out what to do, then executes it.', 2),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)?', 'The part of the CPU that performs calculations and logical comparisons.', 3),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the Control Unit (CU)?', 'The part of the CPU that decodes instructions and sends control signals to coordinate the other components.', 4),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is cache?', 'Very fast memory inside or close to the CPU that stores frequently used instructions and data.', 5),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What are registers?', 'Tiny, very fast storage locations inside the CPU that hold data and instructions currently being processed.', 6),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What does the Program Counter (PC) store?', 'The address of the next instruction to be fetched.', 7),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What does the Memory Address Register (MAR) store?', 'The address in memory of the data or instruction being fetched or stored.', 8),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What does the Memory Data Register (MDR) store?', 'The data or instruction that has been fetched from memory or is about to be written to memory.', 9),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What does the accumulator store?', 'The results of calculations carried out by the ALU.', 10),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is Von Neumann architecture?', 'A design where data and instructions are stored together in the same memory, and the CPU processes them one at a time.', 11),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is clock speed?', 'The number of fetch–decode–execute cycles the CPU can carry out per second, measured in hertz (e.g. 3.5 GHz).', 12),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'How does increasing clock speed affect performance?', 'More instructions can be processed per second, so programs run faster.', 13),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is a core?', 'An independent processing unit within the CPU that can fetch, decode and execute instructions.', 14),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'How does having more cores improve performance?', 'Multiple instructions can be processed at the same time, as long as the software is designed to use them.', 15),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'Why might doubling the number of cores not double performance?', 'Some tasks can''t be split up, and cores must communicate and coordinate, which takes time.', 16),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'How does cache size affect performance?', 'A larger cache can store more data close to the CPU, reducing slower accesses to RAM.', 17),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is an embedded system?', 'A computer system built into a larger device to perform a dedicated function, e.g. in a washing machine or car.', 18),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'Give three examples of embedded systems.', 'Washing machines, microwave ovens, car engine management systems, traffic lights and digital watches.', 19),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What are the characteristics of embedded systems?', 'Dedicated function, small size, low power consumption, low cost, and usually reliable.', 20),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the difference between a general-purpose computer and an embedded system?', 'A general-purpose computer can run many different programs; an embedded system performs one specific task.', 21),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is a bus?', 'A set of wires that carries data, addresses or control signals between components.', 22),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What happens in the fetch stage?', 'The address in the PC is copied to the MAR, the instruction is fetched into the MDR, and the PC is incremented.', 23),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What happens in the decode stage?', 'The control unit works out what the instruction means and what needs to be done.', 24),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What happens in the execute stage?', 'The instruction is carried out, e.g. a calculation in the ALU or data stored in memory.', 25),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'Why is cache faster than RAM?', 'It is physically closer to the CPU and uses faster (more expensive) memory technology.', 26),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is a disadvantage of cache?', 'It is expensive, so only a small amount can be included.', 27),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the purpose of the address bus?', 'To carry memory addresses from the CPU to memory.', 28),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is the purpose of the data bus?', 'To carry data between the CPU, memory and other components.', 29),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.1', 'Systems Architecture', 'What is a GPU?', 'A graphics processing unit, specialised for processing images and parallel tasks.', 30),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is primary storage?', 'Memory that the CPU can access directly, such as RAM and ROM.', 1),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is RAM?', 'Random access memory: volatile memory that stores data, programs and the operating system currently in use.', 2),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What does volatile mean?', 'The contents are lost when the power is turned off.', 3),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is ROM?', 'Read-only memory: non-volatile memory that stores the boot-up instructions (BIOS).', 4),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is the difference between RAM and ROM?', 'RAM is volatile and can be written to; ROM is non-volatile and usually read-only.', 5),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Why do computers need primary storage?', 'To hold data and instructions the CPU needs quickly, because secondary storage is too slow.', 6),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is virtual memory?', 'Part of secondary storage used as extra RAM when RAM is full.', 7),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Why is virtual memory slow?', 'Secondary storage is much slower than RAM, so swapping data in and out slows the computer down.', 8),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is secondary storage?', 'Non-volatile storage used to keep data permanently, e.g. hard drives and SSDs.', 9),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Why do computers need secondary storage?', 'To store files, programs and the operating system permanently when the power is off.', 10),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is magnetic storage?', 'Storage that uses magnetised regions on spinning platters, e.g. a hard disk drive (HDD).', 11),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is optical storage?', 'Storage that uses lasers to read and write data on discs, e.g. CDs, DVDs and Blu-ray.', 12),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'What is solid-state storage?', 'Storage with no moving parts that uses flash memory, e.g. SSDs and USB drives.', 13),
  ('Computer Science', 'OCR', 'gcse', '1.2', 'Memory and Storage', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of magnetic storage.', 'Advantage: high capacity at low cost. Disadvantage: moving parts can be damaged, and it is slower than SSD.', 14),
